WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.570 line:0%
Gav:
<i> Today, we're near Uvalde.</i>

00:00:04.603 --> 00:00:05.805 line:0%
I'm about to fire a tank.

00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:07.507 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
Fire!

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.043 line:0%
- Oh!
- Whoa!

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:11.677
Gav:<i> That was intense.</i>

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.379
Dan:<i> It blew me away.</i>

00:00:13.412 --> 00:00:15.281 align:end position:100%
It's outpacing
that shockwave.

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:17.483
Dan:<i> Oh, there's a shockwave.
 You can see the shockwave.</i>

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:18.684
Gav:<i> I think that's
 some of the coolest footage
 I've ever seen.</i>

00:00:18.717 --> 00:00:20.086
<i> What is this?</i>

00:00:20.119 --> 00:00:22.121
It's my baby. Fire!

00:00:22.154 --> 00:00:24.557
It's an absolute war zone.

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.593 align:start size:88% position:13%
( exhaling )

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.333 size:78% position:39% line:0%
Well, that was, honestly,

00:00:34.366 --> 00:00:36.836 align:start position:0% line:0%
some of the most insane
high-speed footage I've seen.

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:38.738
I think my brain's just
about recovered

00:00:38.771 --> 00:00:40.740
from the shockwaves of that.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:42.341
- It was loud.
- It was loud.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:44.710
The D20 was loud.

00:00:44.743 --> 00:00:46.579 align:start position:0%
Three, two, one, fire!

00:00:51.317 --> 00:00:53.286 align:start size:56% position:44%
Did you hear that?

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:56.222 align:start size:84% position:16%
That was one of my most fun
days I think, actually.

00:00:56.255 --> 00:00:57.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah?
- Firing a tank is awesome.

00:00:57.823 --> 00:00:59.692
When I was a kid
I always wanted to know

00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:01.260
what it'd be like
to be in a tank.

00:01:01.293 --> 00:01:03.596
So we're inside
the Sherman right now.

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:04.864
It's pretty cramped.

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:06.766 align:start size:63% position:38%
It would have
five people usually.

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:08.568 align:start size:56% position:44%
You got the driver
and the co-driver.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.870 align:start size:66% position:34%
You have a commander,
the gunner's seat,

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.338 size:88% position:56%
and you'd also have a loader

00:01:12.371 --> 00:01:14.807 align:start size:59% position:41%
whose only job
is to load the gun.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.277 align:start size:72% position:28%
This here's the breech,
which is where you'd
load the ammunition in.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.680 align:start size:81% position:19%
So you have to punch it in
with your fist

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:22.315
and quickly remove your fist,

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:23.816 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause this part
will come across and lock in

00:01:23.849 --> 00:01:25.485 align:start size:78% position:22%
and then you can fire it.

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:28.721 size:84% position:42%
I can't imagine, especially
with five people in here,

00:01:28.754 --> 00:01:31.624 align:start position:0%
what the change
in pressure must be like

00:01:31.657 --> 00:01:33.559 align:start position:0%
when this thing's
getting hit or shooting.

00:01:33.592 --> 00:01:35.495 align:start size:50% position:50%
Actually being
inside the tank,

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:37.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
you get much less of an effect
when you're firing it.

00:01:37.596 --> 00:01:39.298 align:start size:78% position:22%
It doesn't feel as beefy.

00:01:39.331 --> 00:01:40.666 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because you're outside,
you have all that pressure.

00:01:40.699 --> 00:01:42.235 align:start size:63% position:38%
Whereas inside,
you don't really,

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.469 align:start size:69% position:31%
'cause you got that
breech protecting you,

00:01:43.502 --> 00:01:44.570 align:start size:53% position:47%
and you got steel
all around you.

00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:46.239 align:start position:0%
You know,
people overuse the word,

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:48.241 align:start size:81% position:19%
- but it's awesome.
- Yeah.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:49.876 align:start size:91% position:9%
- It's very cool.
  But how do you drive it?
- Yeah.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:51.677 align:start size:91% position:9%
- There's no wind screen.
- That's fair.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.379
Is there a steering wheel?
Like, what's going on?

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:54.914
I actually would--
I have no idea.

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.782
What even are the controls?
Pedals, buttons, levers?

00:01:56.815 --> 00:01:58.584 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah.
- I just assumed it was
  a thumbstick

00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:00.786
- kind of controller.
- Of course you did.

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:02.221 align:start size:47% position:53%
So we took
the opportunity

00:02:02.254 --> 00:02:03.556 align:start size:63% position:38%
to learn
how to drive a tank.

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:05.224 align:start position:0%
I'm up in the tower.

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:07.793 size:84% position:42%
Okay, I'll try not to crash
or do anything too rash.

00:02:07.826 --> 00:02:09.362
Yeah, try not
to bend the thing.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:11.364 align:start position:0%
All right,
so to start it up,
just click it.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.366 align:start position:0%
Down, yep.

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:15.868 align:start size:50% position:50%
Very satisfying.

00:02:15.901 --> 00:02:18.371 align:start position:0% line:0%
We're gonna
be heading toward...

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:38.158 align:start size:16% position:84% line:0%
Yeah.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.553 align:start size:34% position:66% line:0%
Yeah.

00:03:09.688 --> 00:03:11.257 line:0%
( man laughs )

00:03:27.506 --> 00:03:29.842 align:start position:0% line:0%
Perfect.

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:10.650 align:start size:59% position:41% line:0%
A little shopping--

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:13.019 line:0%
No. Okay.

00:04:52.991 --> 00:04:54.727
Is it fun to drive?

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.762 align:start size:53% position:47%
It's pretty cool.

00:04:56.795 --> 00:04:58.864 align:start size:91% position:9%
- You just have
  these two levers.
- Think I could drive it.

00:04:58.897 --> 00:05:00.800 align:start size:91% position:9%
- I think you could do it.
- Yeah?

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:02.501 size:84% position:58%
I think that even though
you can't even drive a car,

00:05:02.534 --> 00:05:04.036 align:start size:53% position:47%
I think you could
drive a tank.

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:07.340 align:start position:0%
- Don't have a driver's license.
- I think it's easier.

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:10.409 align:start position:0%
Well, now we all know
how to drive a Sherman tank

00:05:10.442 --> 00:05:12.378 align:start size:91% position:9%
- in an emergency.
- Or a zombie apocalypse.

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:13.979 align:start position:0%
Or a zombie apocalypse.

00:05:14.012 --> 00:05:16.982
Next up, to help us learn more
about how a tank works,

00:05:17.015 --> 00:05:18.451
we have an ammunition
technician.

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.053 line:0%
- Dan.
- Hello.

00:05:21.086 --> 00:05:22.822 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Thanks for coming on.
- My pleasure.

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.824 align:start position:0%
What could you tell us
about the tanks
that we were in?

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:28.461
Well, the one we just drove,
that was a jumbo.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.596
There was only 250
of them ever made.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:33.666 align:start position:0%
And you run us through
what actually occurs
in the tank when it fires?

00:05:33.699 --> 00:05:36.535 align:start size:69% position:31%
I can.
This is the projectile

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:38.437 align:start size:59% position:41%
that was fired
by the tank itself.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:40.439
It's about 15
and a half pounds.

00:05:40.472 --> 00:05:41.941
Can't really relate to that.

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:44.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
- It's over a stone.
- Yeah, it's the equivalent

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.146
of three whole bags of flour.

00:05:47.179 --> 00:05:48.981 align:start position:0%
What would you rather
get hit by?

00:05:49.014 --> 00:05:50.816 align:start position:0%
This or three bags
of flour?

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:52.017 align:start size:44% position:56%
Well, I mean,
probably this,

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.719 align:start size:44% position:56%
but at that
sort of speed,

00:05:53.752 --> 00:05:55.054 align:start size:75% position:25%
either one would
probably do some damage.

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:56.589 align:start position:0%
That would just
be a lot more dusty.

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:57.823
- Yeah.
- Poof!

00:05:57.856 --> 00:05:59.392
So what happens is,

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:01.694
if I lay it down
on the side here,

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:03.496 align:start size:69% position:31%
imagine here we've got
the cartridge case.

00:06:03.529 --> 00:06:05.464 align:start size:69% position:31%
What happens
when you fire the tank

00:06:05.497 --> 00:06:07.133 align:start size:59% position:41%
when I was pulling
that little string,

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:10.136 align:start size:81% position:19%
a firing pin was impinging
onto the primer

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:11.537 align:start size:66% position:34%
and that was igniting
the propellant

00:06:11.570 --> 00:06:12.805 align:start size:81% position:19%
inside the cartridge case.

00:06:12.838 --> 00:06:14.607 align:start size:59% position:41%
That propellant
is hitting the base

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.542 align:start size:72% position:28%
of the projectile here.

00:06:16.575 --> 00:06:18.644
You see this part here?
This is called a driving band.

00:06:18.677 --> 00:06:19.979
This is copper.

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:21.547 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So this is softer.
- This is softer.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.048 align:start size:69% position:31%
So if you look down
the barrel of the gun,

00:06:23.081 --> 00:06:26.685 align:start size:69% position:31%
you'll see the rifling
like twisting grooves.

00:06:26.718 --> 00:06:28.821 align:start size:53% position:47%
And because this
is slightly wider

00:06:28.854 --> 00:06:30.623 size:84% position:58%
than the projectile itself,

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:33.826 align:start size:56% position:44%
the copper engages
in the rifling
of the barrel.

00:06:33.859 --> 00:06:38.464 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that then imparts a spin
on the projectile.

00:06:38.497 --> 00:06:41.500 align:start position:0%
So this little strip
is the reason
it's coming out like this.

00:06:41.533 --> 00:06:43.502 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Yeah. And I'll show you
  what I mean.
- Okay.

00:06:43.535 --> 00:06:45.905
This is one
that we've previously fired.

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:48.641 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Obviously--
- Can you guess
  which has been fired?

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.776 align:start size:53% position:47%
Yeah, we dug this
out of the ground

00:06:50.809 --> 00:06:53.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you can actually see these
individual grooves here

00:06:53.612 --> 00:06:56.982
are the copper that have gone
through the rifling there.

00:06:57.015 --> 00:06:58.784
This engages in the teeth
of the barrel

00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:01.120
and then causes the projectile
to spin when it comes out.

00:07:01.153 --> 00:07:02.888 align:start size:50% position:50%
Every ten inches
it travels,

00:07:02.921 --> 00:07:05.458 align:start size:63% position:38%
it's done
a complete rotation.

00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:06.792
So to clearly illustrate
what I'm talking about

00:07:06.825 --> 00:07:08.227 align:start size:47% position:53%
we can just see
the footage.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.930
This is my favorite shot.

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:13.899
Gav:<i> So almost as it travels
 the length of itself,</i>

00:07:13.932 --> 00:07:16.235
<i> it's coming round
 on a full rotation.</i>

00:07:16.268 --> 00:07:18.037 align:start position:0%
- Dan:<i> Yeah. </i>
- Gav:<i> So if it wasn't spinning,</i>

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:19.905
<i> that would just be tumbling
 through the air.</i>

00:07:19.938 --> 00:07:23.909
<i> And you can definitely see
 the rotation from the rifling.</i>

00:07:23.942 --> 00:07:25.244
Dan:<i> It's like
 an American football.</i>

00:07:25.277 --> 00:07:26.812
<i> When you throw one,
 you spin it</i>

00:07:26.845 --> 00:07:28.681 align:start position:0%
-<i> and it stabilizes in flight. </i>
-<i> Yeah.</i>

00:07:28.714 --> 00:07:31.750
<i> So, obviously,
 1,700 feet per second</i>

00:07:31.783 --> 00:07:34.086 align:start size:84% position:16%
-<i> is faster than
 the speed of sound. </i>
-<i> Yeah.</i>

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:36.121
<i> Which is why we've got these
 bow waves you can see
 in the air.</i>

00:07:36.154 --> 00:07:38.724
We actually got very lucky
with the fact that this is,

00:07:38.757 --> 00:07:41.627
you know, it's a gradient
from blue to a darker color.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.096
<i> You can see it very clearly</i>

00:07:44.129 --> 00:07:45.764
<i> 'cause you got two colors
 coming together.</i>

00:07:45.797 --> 00:07:47.700
<i> Which I've never seen before</i>

00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:49.702
<i> with, like,
 naked eye, obviously.</i>

00:07:49.735 --> 00:07:51.036
<i> That's very cool.</i>

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:52.738 align:start position:0%
Holding it,
and it's so dense.

00:07:52.771 --> 00:07:55.274 align:start position:0%
It's like, wow,
that is seriously damaging.

00:07:55.307 --> 00:07:56.809
That one was 15 pounds,

00:07:56.842 --> 00:08:01.847
but the one we fired
from the 152 mil

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:03.582
D20. Hold this.

00:08:03.615 --> 00:08:05.584 align:start size:75% position:25%
- There you go.
- Yeah.

00:08:05.617 --> 00:08:06.986 align:start position:0%
That's a lot heavier.

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:08.554 align:start size:63% position:38%
It's about half
your weight I think.

00:08:08.587 --> 00:08:10.890 align:start size:66% position:34%
This shell actually
comes out really fast

00:08:10.923 --> 00:08:14.059 align:start size:59% position:41%
even though the way
it defeats armor
is very different.

00:08:14.092 --> 00:08:17.630 align:start size:91% position:9%
This used the power and speed
to penetrate the armor.

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:18.898 align:start size:50% position:50%
Whereas this
has an explosive
charge in it,

00:08:18.931 --> 00:08:20.266 align:start size:78% position:22%
which is what damages it.

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:21.700 align:start size:63% position:38%
It used a completely
different method

00:08:21.733 --> 00:08:22.968 align:start size:72% position:28%
to penetrate the tanks.

00:08:23.001 --> 00:08:25.137 align:start position:0%
And we actually found
some of the remnants.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.906 align:start size:22% position:78%
We did.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:28.941 align:start size:53% position:47%
We dug this out
of the earth bank
in the back.

00:08:28.974 --> 00:08:31.043 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So this is our one.
- Yeah.

00:08:33.045 --> 00:08:34.780 align:start position:0%
Just crumpled.

00:08:34.813 --> 00:08:37.182 align:start position:0%
The force that has
to do that is mental.

00:08:37.215 --> 00:08:39.618 align:start position:0%
Well, thank you very much,
Dan, for teaching me

00:08:39.651 --> 00:08:42.588 align:start size:97% position:3%
- a lot about how tanks work.
- My pleasure.

00:08:42.621 --> 00:08:44.590 align:start position:0%
Well, hopefully you enjoyed
that episode. I did.

00:08:44.623 --> 00:08:47.092 align:start position:0%
Feel free to subscribe
to the Slow Mo Guys over here,

00:08:47.125 --> 00:08:48.627 align:start position:0%
and make sure you check out
other episodes

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:50.963 align:start position:0%
of "Planet Slow Mo".

00:08:50.996 --> 00:08:53.699 align:start size:66% position:34% line:0%
- What?
- Nothing.

